00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:05.680
in a picture of a signature,
just to know what it looks like

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:06.850
and what their code is,

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:10.603
and then notary stamp gets
you unlimited amount of money.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:12.723
I walk out with...

00:12:12.723 --> 00:12:14.770
I walked out with 15
laptops the other day.

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.580
- [Mark] Oh, you're basically stealing.

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:19.000
- Yeah, but walking right
out in front of them,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:20.799
not even trying to hide it.

00:12:20.799 --> 00:12:22.230
In a suit and tie.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:23.063
- [Mark] Right.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.593
Yeah. It's incredible.

00:12:28.037 --> 00:12:30.630
- [Mark] [Indistinct] downtown
and hit those buildings-

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:31.910
- And then come down to Skid Row

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:34.569
and get rid of them for
pennies on the dollar.

00:12:34.569 --> 00:12:35.402
- [Mark] Right.

00:12:35.402 --> 00:12:36.235
- Anonymously.

00:12:38.234 --> 00:12:39.180
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.030
- Yeah, it's a lot of work.

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.880
I mean I got cellulitis in my leg.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:43.700
I probably need to get my leg amputated

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.563
if I don't like keep taking care of it.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.414
- [Mark] But...

00:12:49.414 --> 00:12:51.520
So, I mean, do you have hopes

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:53.660
of getting back together
with your kids or your-

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:54.610
- That's...

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:57.240
Honestly, that's what I'm doing this for.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:58.804
It sounds crazy, but I hit...

00:12:58.804 --> 00:13:02.200
I came upon quarter million
dollars in gold last year

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.043
and I fucked it all off.

00:13:05.043 --> 00:13:07.360
I didn't realize what I
had cause I was impatient

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.763
and I was also,

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:12.010
you know, playing the game, they call it.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:12.843
And

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:18.401
- [Mark] What do you mean you...

00:13:18.401 --> 00:13:22.210
How'd you come across $250,000 of-

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:23.962
- I broke into a house.

00:13:23.962 --> 00:13:24.795
- [Mark] You broke into a house?

00:13:24.795 --> 00:13:26.990
- And I don't really ever do that.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.600
Does that explained to
women's watch you're wearing

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:30.800
on your hand? (laughs)

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.020
- No, this came out of a car.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:33.370
- [Mark] Oh, is it?

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:34.203
- Sadly.

00:13:34.203 --> 00:13:35.036
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:13:35.036 --> 00:13:36.540
- I don't, again, it's
not like I break windows.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:38.690
I don't like to do any
of that kind of shit

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.380
but like a Rolex only got me 400 bucks

00:13:42.649 --> 00:13:44.963
and in San Francisco of
all places, you know?

00:13:49.286 --> 00:13:50.243
You know,

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.940
I like to play Robin Hood.
Like I go and I take advantage

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:58.340
of this white privilege
shit because it is real.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:00.530
And I see people out here

00:14:02.434 --> 00:14:03.750
who genuinely

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:06.380
truly try their hardest

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:08.403
to survive

00:14:08.403 --> 00:14:09.330
and not just to survive,

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:13.380
but to keep a level head and to like...

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:17.200
It's inspiring to see how
some of these people can live

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.600
and keep a positive mindset

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.960
because I've always been
a really positive person

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.820
and going through like my
trials and tribulations,

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.260
I like being happy.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.010
And I like when the world is happy too.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:31.150
And like we're all in a harmony, you know?

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:33.380
And it sucks to have to be that guy.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:36.330
But you know, I do
pretty good about keeping

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.920
my guilt from like,
getting to me, you know?

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:40.320
It's like once every three weeks or so,

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:41.820
I'll break down,

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.470
because my guilt, my
conscience will just...

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:44.650
it's not like I sleep at night

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.690
and have nightmares or nothing,

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:50.240
but I've died six times
in my life, you know?

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.700
Been brought back.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:52.650
- [Mark] From what?

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.770
- Accidental overdose

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:57.750
cause I don't use needles.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:59.850
Just smoking this fentanyl
shit here in town.

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:02.350
It's the most deadly thing
I've ever seen in my life.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.470
I've been smoking drugs forever.

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:05.820
I hit it one time a week ago, I hit it.

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:06.660
- [Mark] Super dangerous.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.253
One hit killed me.

00:15:10.765 --> 00:15:13.343
And then purposeful overdose,
I've done that twice.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:16.200
I hated myself.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:18.443
I fell in love and I
wasn't able to be a man

00:15:18.443 --> 00:15:20.700
that I feel like I deserve to be.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.270
So I tried killing myself
and it didn't work.

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:25.113
So stopped trying that.

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:28.720
I died from exhaustion.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.920
I was climbing buildings and

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:35.090
rappelling through the
skylights and stealing safes.

00:15:38.285 --> 00:15:40.170
And all by myself in the
middle of the pouring rain,

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:42.933
I broke two ribs in my back
and suffocating myself.

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:47.597
Yeah. Rappelling off a building, slipped.

00:15:47.597 --> 00:15:49.600
I also...

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.563
just a few other ways, you know, like.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:54.970
But my heart gave out

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.450
this one time and I had a felony warrant

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:02.600
and I was so spooked that
I was gonna go to jail.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.170
And I was going through
a really rough time

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.430
in my heroin addiction

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.030
and I was dope sick.

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:09.230
And I was looking for anything,

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:12.010
anything to help me get that next fix.

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:15.010
And I'm riding my bike down
this international Boulevard

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:17.140
or Pacific highway that goes all the way

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:19.770
from Mexico to Canada, pretty much.

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:22.128
And I was driving on Pacific highway

00:16:22.128 --> 00:16:25.600
and I felt like this weird
flutter and my right leg

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.390
had been acting up, like
swelling up and stuff.

00:16:28.390 --> 00:16:30.600
So my right leg had swollen up

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.600
probably got two thirds at the time,

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.410
the size it is now. Like
one and two third size.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.100
And my left arm went
numb, started tingling.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.590
And all of a sudden I like collapsed.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.070
And I had like no energy, couldn't move.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:44.460
And that was the first
time I really did die.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.773
Like, and it scared the out of me.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:51.510
And I had a dream about it twice that week

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.060
that I was gonna die.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:58.060
Yeah. And next thing
you know, bam, I'm dead.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:16:59.757
I wake up to this guy going like this,

00:16:59.757 --> 00:17:01.210
you know, chest convulsions or whatever.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:02.673
Boom. I hopped up.

00:17:03.615 --> 00:17:05.290
I was like thinking it
was going to be the EMT.

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:06.623
Now I just got up and ran.

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:08.530
Didn't even care what direction.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.150
I left my bike, everything.
I just fucking ran.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.000
And I made about 20 feet
and then I collapsed,

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:14.833
hit the ground again.

00:17:14.833 --> 00:17:15.690
He's like, "Relax, relax."

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.290
You know, I was like "Oh, shit."

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.180
And I was all scuffed up
cause I was riding a bike,

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:21.740
and when I collapsed, you know,

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:24.174
bars went sideways and I fell and stuff.

00:17:24.174 --> 00:17:27.163
And so I'm bleeding on
my face and I was like...

00:17:27.163 --> 00:17:29.092
And he's like, "I called
the ambulance about..."

00:17:29.092 --> 00:17:31.620
I was like, "How long?", you know.

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.830
About seven minutes ago.
I was like, "Oh, shit,

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.620
it's time to go." You know?

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:38.410
And so I grabbed my stuff
and got back up to Seattle,

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:40.420
got my bill fixed or
whatever with the shit I had.

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:43.914
But it was a pretty traumatic experience.

00:17:43.914 --> 00:17:45.930
- [Mark] What would cause that you think?

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:47.420
The drug you're using-

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:48.940
- Exhaustion,

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:50.390
malnourishment,

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:51.303
lack of sleep,

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:54.530
lack of...

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:57.590
Yeah, and the drugs, you know,

00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:01.010
affecting heart rate and
blood pressure or whatever.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:04.240
- [Mark] Did you think your
upbringing, your childhood,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.320
your mom's addiction, all
of your family addiction

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:10.373
is kind of what kind of
put you on this path?

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:12.570
- In a weird way, yeah.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:16.770
I know the difference between
right and wrong, you know,

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.340
being that I grew up around a lot of bad

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:20.470
and then what's, you know,

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:21.720
what school and everything was trying

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.030
to teach me what's right.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.923
So like going back and forth,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.400
my uncle,

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:29.190
he's a big influence of mine.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.290
Like I remember it was
like the first memories

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.650
of my life going to my
grandma's house, hanging out.

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.290
My mom's like slumped out,

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.610
been asleep for three, four days,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.180
and everybody's partying at my grandma's

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.651
downstairs basement getting high and shit.

00:18:42.651 --> 00:18:45.410
And I was a kid, you know,
three years old, four years old.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:46.410
And I'm watching my uncle come in

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.470
with a stack of hundred
dollar bills like that.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.420
He's like, "Austin.

00:18:50.966 --> 00:18:52.020
Help me count this."

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:52.926
You know?

00:18:52.926 --> 00:18:55.030
And I'm like, "Okay", you know.

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:56.160
As a kid, like, "Heck yeah,

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:59.610
let me get some." You know,
he's like, "No, no no."

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.670
Well, my grandma runs a state business

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:04.690
takes care of developmentally
disabled people.

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:06.720
And she's an angel,

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.800
she's an angel, but

00:19:10.983 --> 00:19:12.000
you know,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.870
that doesn't pay the bills to be an angel.

00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:16.680
And so the business that she was running

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:19.600
was in a wealthier part
of the Seattle East side

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:22.750
and my uncle going to school or

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:24.430
because he'd get hand me
down clothes and stuff.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:25.640
And these people were driving around

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.270
and BMWs in freshman year, you know?

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:32.480
And he got made fun of, and
he's like, got tired of it.

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:33.860
I'm just taking a stand for myself.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:35.670
So, you know, he did.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:36.503
He would...

00:19:36.503 --> 00:19:37.430
He had the gift of the gab.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.110
He would hustle these
people out of everything

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.840
with just words.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.503
And he's clean cut, you know.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:50.000
Clean cut dude and then it became...

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.400
Next thing you know, my grandma's house

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.343
is getting raided by the feds.

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:56.570
And he got caught with a
pound of meth at 19 years old

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:58.591
in the late 90s.

00:19:58.591 --> 00:19:59.424
- [Mark] (indistinct)

00:19:59.424 --> 00:20:00.257
- At 19. Yeah.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:04.280
- [Mark] It sounds like your
family kind of...(laughs)

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:06.890
It was not maybe the
best influence I know.

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:08.103
- No. Yeah, no.

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:10.663
And it's like,

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:12.393
I don't...

00:20:12.393 --> 00:20:13.890
I know that what the outcome is.

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:17.200
My uncle spent 20 years
in prison on and off.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.310
I have no relationship with him.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:22.610
I've seen him beat the hell out of some

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:26.083
people, you know, women and men.

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:28.813
And

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:31.810
I don't want to be that guy.

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:34.063
There's no need.

00:20:34.063 --> 00:20:35.610
I want to do something that like changes

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:36.610
the world, you know?

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:43.823
Unity. That's like brings
unity, a true unity, you know?

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.270
People always take my
kindness for weakness.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:48.920
I'm a good guy. I'm a kind guy

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:49.753
but

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:52.880
I'm also the fucking most fucked up

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:54.213
person in the world, dude.

00:20:55.073 --> 00:20:57.750
These people who do work hard is...

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:58.960
I feel a lot of guilt

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:01.410
because sometimes I get
into places and I'm like,

00:21:03.027 --> 00:21:05.677
"Oh, shit. This person works
every day of their life.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:08.140
They get up every day

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:12.700
and they work their asses off

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:14.750
to just try and provide
for their families."

00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:16.120
And, I didn't even realize what I did

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:17.190
until it's way too late.

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:20.600
I'm like, "Damn. I took
all this from these really,

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:23.003
probably good people that would've

00:21:23.003 --> 00:21:25.303
give you the clothes off
their back." You know?

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.610
And it's like, that's
what I want to change.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.120
I just want to hit that one big one.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:33.190
Hit the lottery one time.

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:35.570
Bam. And be able to just give, you know,

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:37.920
Take a little bit for
myself to be comfortable,

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:39.821
and to...

00:21:39.821 --> 00:21:43.540
Be able to finance my dreams, you know,

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.480
my endeavors.

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:47.950
But I also want to give
back to the, you know,

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:52.950
people who truly do deserve
it, you know? (sniffs)

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.640
- [Mark] Austin, thank you so much for

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:55.473
talking to me.

00:21:55.473 --> 00:21:56.420
- Thank you. This kind of...

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:57.743
I'm not used to this.

00:21:59.110 --> 00:21:59.943
Thank you.

00:21:59.943 --> 00:22:01.121
- [Mark] Yeah. I'm sure you haven't shared

00:22:01.121 --> 00:22:03.617
a lot of this before, right?

00:22:03.617 --> 00:22:05.567
- No, I mean, I preach it every day but

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:10.743
people don't really care, it seems like.

00:22:10.743 --> 00:22:13.380
It seems like a lot of them out here,

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.724
they respect you, you know?

00:22:15.724 --> 00:22:17.162
- [Mark] Not in this neighborhood.

00:22:17.162 --> 00:22:18.472
It's hard to get anybody...

00:22:18.472 --> 00:22:20.103
Anyone's ear down here.

00:22:24.542 --> 00:22:25.840
- Yeah.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.330
But if it saved, man, that'd be so cool.

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:29.180
Like my daughter.

00:22:30.163 --> 00:22:31.970
I got to go turn myself in.

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:34.018
I'm going to turn myself in on

00:22:34.018 --> 00:22:36.580
a bunch of the dumb I've done

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:38.390
because I need to get my health back.

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:39.920
I need to get a real surgeon.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:43.320
Like Seattle is really
known for their healthcare.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:46.000
Get my leg fixed, but I
have to bite the bullet

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.670
and man up for the dumb ass shit I did.

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:50.830
- [Mark] Have you done prison time?

00:22:50.830 --> 00:22:53.650
- No, but I've spent quite a
bit in jail and I looked at...

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:55.000
I'm looking at prison time.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:57.589
- [Mark] So if you're turning yourself in,

00:22:57.589 --> 00:22:59.320
that means prison time?

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:01.019
- Quite possibly, yeah.

00:23:01.019 --> 00:23:03.640
But I'm willing to
sacrifice that to make sure

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.540
that I can be there in the later years

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:09.703
because I actually care
about my life now, you know?

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:13.170
I just wish that one day I could just go

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.160
